[Given unintuitive way of `Deno.run()` API usage](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/4568#issuecomment-772463496) I think we should consider changing that API for Deno 2.0.

Tokio provides a great abstraction for running subprocess by the means of [`Command`](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Command.html) and [`Child`](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Child.html) APIs
especially if user wants to just [run process to completion](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Command.html#method.status) or [run it to completion while collecting output](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Command.html#method.output).

Currently Deno APIs requires users to handle both situations manually; and I believe it's worth considering to add some helper methods.

Hereby I propose to align Deno's subprocess APIs to follow Tokio's API as closely as possible.

```typescript

// run process to completion
const command = new Deno.Command({
    cmd: ["ls"],
});
const status = await command.status();

// run process to completion, collecting output (will configure stdout/stderr to be piped automatically)
const command = new Deno.Command({
    cmd: ["ls"],
});
const { status, stdout, stderr } = await command.output();

// spawn a subprocess and return a handle to it
const command = new Deno.Command({
    cmd: ["ls"],
});
const subprocess = command.spawn();

// wait for subprocess to complete
const status = await subprocess.wait();

// check if subprocess has completed
const maybeStatus = subprocess.tryWait(); // rename to status() ?

// wait for subproces to complete and collect output
const { status, stdout, stderr } = await subprocess.waitWithOutput();

// force subprocess to exit
await subprocess.kill();
```

With the proposed changes to the API all appropriate resources used to facilitate subprocess
would be automatically cleaned up (especially stdio resources), meaning that for the most popular
use cases users wouldn't have to remember to call `Deno.close()` on the resources. 

For more advanced use cases (eg. streaming data from stdout/stderr) there wouldn't be much difference
in the API usability besides changing API names.

